$DIR/main.p:8:3: cannot pass &T{...} to put, which keeps it after this iteration: the loop at line 7 makes it in the same memory in every iteration
    A composite literal whose address is taken (at main.p:8) is made in main's
    frame, as a local variable would be.
    Every iteration of the loop at line 7 reuses that memory. put stores it in
    ps[i] (main.p:4). When the next iteration starts, what put kept would see
    that iteration's value, not this one's.
    Go gives each iteration of a loop its own variables, so a function literal
    made in one iteration keeps that iteration's values. Plutonium keeps
    variables in the function's frame, which has room for one copy of each;
    giving every iteration its own copy would need memory for each iteration,
    allocated behind the program's back.
    Change put so it does not keep the view, or pass a copy of the value instead
    of a view of it.
    Or allocate the T in each iteration with new, and keep its owner, an
    owned[T]:
        p := new(T{...})
    (see "Loop iterations reuse their storage" in the Plutonium spec, section
    18.4)
    (run "pluto explain E3006" for a longer explanation)
